uh more on our top story the government  defending its 1.4 billion  programme to help children across  england catch up on lost  learning caused by the pandemic let's  speak to the shadow education secretary  kate green good morning to you i mean  the education  secretary tells us this is the beginning  of a true revolution  it's a very slow revolution it's um a  revolution that falls  woefully inadequate of what the  government's own education experts or  kevin collins is suggesting  is needed and i'm afraid it badly lets  down our children and young people  um it's going to bring in some extra  tutoring it's going to  bring in some training for teachers but  we know that  parents say that the most uh worrying  concern that they have following the  pandemic is their children's emotional  well-being their ability to  socialize with other children and and  their mental well-being so  it's a very limited announcement i'm  afraid that the government is making  and children and young people can't  really afford to wait for this  government  to get a sensible package that will  properly address  children's educational recovery and  their well-being  yes but in terms of of educational  recovery which  which presumably is most people's  priority here  um shouldn't that be the focus rather  than  um looking at the emotional state of  children i mean  i know not all but a lot of them do just  bounce back when things get back to  normal  so it's very clear that good learning  depends on good emotional and mental  well-being if you're worried if you're  frightened if you're withdrawn  and if you're not engaging with teachers  and other students then that is going to  inhibit your good learning and that's  why we say learning and wellbeing go  hand in hand and in fact  parents say and teachers say that it is  their top priority to address  children's mental well-being as a  prerequisite really for  good quality learning and for children's  happiness and their development  we also know very worryingly that and  kevin collins himself has identified  and arise in mental health problems  among children and young people  so this can't be an either or of course  we've got to help children recover lost  learning and it is worrying that  the gap entertainment between better off  children and more disadvantaged children  is widening and that has to be addressed  but  good quality mental health services and  social activities  the chance to play in development are  going to be equally important  and just not provided for in the  government's proposals yeah but 1.4  billion pounds announced today 1.7  billion pounds an ounce  at the end of last year i mean 3.1  billion in total  it's a lot of money  it's well short of what kevin collins  himself has said  is needed and of course the investment  we make in  children and young people today clearly  clearly crucially important for them but  it's also  really important for our country's  future success and prosperity  so we've seen um economic think tanks  like the institute for physical studies  suggesting that a  failure to um enable children to catch  up on lost learning and and recover from  the pandemic will cost hundreds of  billions of pounds to our  our country down the line so not making  this investment up front in children and  young people now  not only is that morally wrong we really  do have to prioritize our young people  it's also a false economy and i'm afraid  is the wrong choice  not to be prioritizing children's  learning and well-being  i mean the education secretary said this  morning they are prioritizing children  they're looking at increasing using this  money to increase one-on-one tuition  to catch up on lost learning to cover  six million students  their announcement today will actually  amount to an hour a fortnight of  tutoring for each pupil so i'm not sure  it's as bold and ambitious as the  education secretary  might like to suggest but as i've been  saying simply doing 